# R10 Part 1 proposed fix — Toggle / Slider iOS facade migration

## Goal

Replace the SwiftUI Toggle/Slider hosted in UIViewRepresentable with a
direct UIViewRepresentable wrapping UIKit UISwitch / UISlider, so
XCUIElement.tap() (Toggle) and the existing .adjust(...) (Slider) both
route through documented Apple control internals that fire
UIControlEventValueChanged.

## Scope

iOS only (`#if canImport(UIKit)`). macOS Toggle/Slider facades untouched.
ToggleOverrides / SliderOverrides / BoolStorage / DoubleStorage / Crystal
populators unchanged.

## Files touched (no API breakage)

1. swift/.../ToggleFacade.swift
   - Add `APSKToggleRepresentable: UIViewRepresentable` wrapping `UISwitch`.
   - Replace the body of `ToggleHost.body` on iOS to use that representable
     (instead of `SwiftUI.Toggle`). Keep the macOS branch unchanged.
   - Coordinator stores BoolStorage + actionToken; fires
     CallbackBridge.fire on UIControlEventValueChanged.
   - applyOverrides(to:) helper called from both makeUIView and
     updateUIView (covers reactive Crystal-side property updates AND
     initial state).
   - Programmatic state mirror: in updateUIView, if `sw.isOn !=
     storage.value`, call `sw.setOn(storage.value, animated: false)` —
     UIKit setters do not re-fire valueChanged, so no callback loop.
   - Crystal-side `apsk_toggle_set_value` path is unchanged (still writes
     `storage.value = X`; storage's @Published machinery re-invokes
     updateUIView; the equality check makes the mirror idempotent).

2. swift/.../SliderFacade.swift
   - Add `APSKSliderRepresentable: UIViewRepresentable` wrapping
     `UISlider`.
   - Replace the body of `SliderDoubleHost.body` on iOS to use that
     representable. Continuous (no step) and stepped variants supported.
   - Coordinator: on valueChanged, if step is set, quantize before
     writing to DoubleStorage; reassign sender.value to the snapped value
     so the thumb visually snaps; always fire CallbackBridge.fire.
   - In updateUIView: apply min/max/value/step/isEnabled from overrides
     every update. Mirror Crystal-side programmatic state via
     `sender.setValue(value, animated: false)` when not equal.

3. Note: The current iOS Slider visit in uikit_renderer.cr builds an
   ELABORATE synthetic-track structure for screenshot stability (a
   container UIView with track/fill/thumb subviews PLUS the real UISlider
   at alpha 0.0 on top). That path is OUTSIDE the SwiftKit facade — it
   happens in Crystal. R10 leaves that path UNTOUCHED. The migration here
   only affects code paths that take the SwiftKit
   `apsk_make_slider_reactive` entry — namely the SliderFacade Swift
   side. Since R10's Slider migration is parity/robustness (BX4 already
   PASSES via XCUIElement.adjust on the synthetic-track UISlider), this
   is fine.

## Memory / lifecycle

- `BoolStorage` / `DoubleStorage` retains unchanged; Crystal still owns
  the +1 retain acquired in the @make_reactive trampoline.
- `Coordinator` is held by the UIViewRepresentable's Context — standard
  SwiftUI pattern. No new retain cycles.

## Regression risk

- macOS: UNCHANGED (skip the iOS branch).
- iOS BX1 (Button): untouched — Button is a discrete-action control on
  its own facade.
- iOS BX4 (Slider): currently PASSES. Risk: SliderHost's iOS body now
  produces a UIViewRepresentable wrapper instead of SwiftUI.Slider — but
  the iOS path uses the synthetic-track in uikit_renderer that paints
  over the Swift facade's UISlider. Need to verify the synthetic-track
  still finds the UISlider under the new representable. The
  representable's UISlider IS the makeUIView return value, so a top-down
  subview walk still finds it.
- iOS reactive setter (UI::Toggle#is_on=, UI::Slider#value=): unchanged
  end-to-end. The Crystal-side calls `apsk_toggle_set_value(state,
  value)` which mutates `storage.value`; storage triggers SwiftUI
  re-evaluation; the new representable's `updateUIView` mirrors the new
  value onto the UISwitch / UISlider.
- iOS Toggle visual styling: brief explicitly says "Do NOT apply glass /
  visual treatment to UISwitch / UISlider in R10. Default UIKit
  appearance is fine." So we use bare UISwitch / UISlider here.

## Suppression / suppressNextFire

The existing `suppressNextFire` flag in BoolStorage / DoubleStorage was
designed for SwiftUI Toggle's `.onChange` consumer. In the UIKit-wrapper
path, programmatic state updates flow through `updateUIView` (NOT through
the coordinator's @objc valueChanged), so the suppression flag is not
needed for the new path. We leave it in place (the macOS facade still
uses it).

## Test expectation

testBX3_toggleValueCallback should now PASS — XCUIElement.tap() on the
UISwitch routes through UIControl's tap-handling, which fires
valueChanged, which fires CallbackBridge.fire and updates storage.value.

testBX4_sliderValueCallback should remain PASSING (parity migration).
